.TH GIT-CLEANUP-REPO 1 "2026-07-31" "git-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
git-cleanup-repo \- delete safely merged local branches
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git cleanup-repo
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git cleanup-repo
resolves the remote fetch endpoint, fetches the exact repository default branch
without updating remote-tracking refs or FETCH_HEAD, then deletes stale local
branches whose exact object IDs are proven merged.
.PP
By default it deletes local branches that are already merged into the base
branch. This includes squash-, rebase-, and cherry-pick-merged branches: patch
IDs identify candidate matches, and the exact net tree delta must also be
present in a pinned base snapshot. A clean squash merge is recognized even
though its commits are not ancestors of the base, while byte-, mode-, or later
same-path differences are excluded. Branch and upstream-state enumeration must
complete and validate before the first deletion. Each candidate is rechecked,
and its ref is deleted only if it still has the exact proven object ID. It can
also delete branches whose existing upstream tracking state is gone or every
local branch except the base branch. The command does not execute configured
fetch or prune mappings; other remote-tracking state must be refreshed
separately when needed.
.PP
Base selection does not depend on configured fetch refspecs or short ref names.
The command fetches exactly
.BR refs/heads/ base
without tags, validates and pins its native SHA-1 or SHA-256 object ID, and
checks the local fast-forward relationship before switching branches. It then
creates or fast-forwards the base using that same object ID. A divergent base
fails before the current branch or local base is changed. Dry-run uses isolated
temporary object storage.
.PP
The command retains the single raw configured fetch URL for both remote
inspection and fetch, allowing Git to apply any
.B insteadOf
rewrite exactly once per operation. Remotes with zero or multiple fetch URLs
are rejected. Git 2.29 or newer is required.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-base " " \fIbranch\fR
Base branch to keep and update.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-remote " " \fIname\fR
Remote fetch endpoint to resolve and fetch from. Defaults to
.BR origin .
.TP
.B \-\-gone
Also delete branches whose upstream is gone.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Delete all local branches except the base branch.
.TP
.B \-\-remove\-worktrees
Remove clean, operation-free linked worktrees for branches selected for
deletion. Removal never uses
.BR \-\-force .
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print actions without changing anything.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.SH SAFETY
The command refuses to run with a dirty current worktree or active rebase,
merge, cherry-pick, or revert state. A linked worktree is preserved when it has
uncommitted, ignored, or index-hidden local content, is unreadable, is locked by
an active operation, or cannot be removed without force. Git cannot atomically
combine worktree reservation checks with ref deletion, so callers must not
create, switch, or mutate worktrees concurrently with cleanup. Observable state
is rechecked, and expected-old-object-ID ref deletion preserves a branch that
advances during cleanup.
